    <data name="Shell_CropHelpHeading.Text" xml:space="preserve">
        <value>Rotation and edge trimming</value>
    </data>
    <data name="Shell_CropHelpIntroduction.Text" xml:space="preserve">
        <value>JPEG stores image details in blocks. Depending on how colour samples are stored, these are grouped into larger coding units. The right or bottom edge can contain an incomplete unit when the image dimensions do not align with that grid.</value>
    </data>
    <data name="Shell_CropHelpRotation.Text" xml:space="preserve">
        <value>A rotation can move an incomplete edge to a position that cannot be transformed perfectly in the coefficient domain. This original illustration shows why rotating only the complete region would leave an incorrect strip.</value>
    </data>
    <data name="Shell_CropHelpTrimming.Text" xml:space="preserve">
        <value>Trimming removes the affected source-edge pixels so the remaining image can be rotated without re-quantizing its JPEG coefficients. Those removed pixels are lost from the result: trimming is not preservation of the complete image.</value>
    </data>
    <data name="Shell_CropHelpChoices.Text" xml:space="preserve">
        <value>Keep all image edges skips images whose requested transform would require trimming. Allow edge trimming requires you to review and confirm the exact discarded right and bottom source-edge pixels for each affected image. Choosing the setting alone never grants that consent.</value>
    </data>
    <data name="Shell_CropSourceImage.[using:Microsoft.UI.Xaml.Automation]AutomationProperties.Name" xml:space="preserve">
        <value>Original illustration with a partial edge region</value>
    </data>
    <data name="Shell_CropRotatedImage.[using:Microsoft.UI.Xaml.Automation]AutomationProperties.Name" xml:space="preserve">
        <value>Illustration of an incomplete region after rotation</value>
    </data>
    <data name="Shell_CropResultImage.[using:Microsoft.UI.Xaml.Automation]AutomationProperties.Name" xml:space="preserve">
        <value>Illustration of rotation after the affected edge has been trimmed</value>
    </data>
    <data name="Shell_ProgressiveHelpHeading.Text" xml:space="preserve">
        <value>Progressive JPEG</value>
    </data>
    <data name="Shell_ProgressiveHelpIntroduction.Text" xml:space="preserve">
        <value>A progressive JPEG stores its coefficients across several scans. A compatible viewer can show a rough preview before later scans add detail. A sequential JPEG delivers coefficients in sequential scans instead.</value>
    </data>
    <data name="Shell_ProgressiveHelpExample.Text" xml:space="preserve">
        <value>Use the carousel arrows, swipe, or press the left and right arrow keys to compare the first, middle and final detail levels of the original example. Windows decodes the actual progressive levels.</value>
    </data>
    <data name="Shell_ProgressiveHelpChoices.Text" xml:space="preserve">
        <value>Keep existing scan organization preserves the source arrangement. Sequential and Progressive explicitly choose a new arrangement for the output. Compatibility and file size depend on the image and the software reading it; there is no guaranteed percentage saving.</value>
    </data>
    <data name="Shell_ProgressiveHelpQuality.Text" xml:space="preserve">
        <value>These choices do not re-quantize JPEG coefficients or increase image quality. Rotation may still require edge trimming as explained separately. Changing an option invalidates an existing review and requires a fresh analysis.</value>
    </data>
    <data name="Shell_ProgressiveExampleImage.[using:Microsoft.UI.Xaml.Automation]AutomationProperties.Name" xml:space="preserve">
        <value>Original progressive JPEG example, fully decoded</value>
    </data>
    <data name="Shell_PrivacyHeading.Text" xml:space="preserve">
        <value>JPG Spinner privacy notice</value>
    </data>
    <data name="Shell_PrivacyProcessing.Text" xml:space="preserve">
        <value>The app reads the folders you select, including photo content and metadata such as orientation, camera information and potentially location or other personal information. It uses this information to analyze and correct image orientation and preserve supported metadata. Image analysis does not change your files. Processing writes corrected copies, or replaces originals only through the reviewed backup workflow. The app does not send photos or metadata to Hadden Industries.</value>
    </data>
    <data name="Shell_PrivacyLocalData.Text" xml:space="preserve">
        <value>Processing preferences and retained Windows folder-access permissions are stored under this app's local identity. Recovery journals contain file identities, paths and transaction facts needed to recover interrupted work. Originals, corrected copies, verified backups and journals are operational data, not sanitized diagnostic logs. They are not covered by diagnostic rotation; backups do not expire automatically.</value>
    </data>
    <data name="Shell_PrivacyDiagnostics.Text" xml:space="preserve">
        <value>To help diagnose failures, the app records the UTC time, app version and architecture, a random per-operation identifier, processing stage, error code, elapsed time and resource-limit information when available. Missing optional values are recorded as null. The identifier groups one operation; it is not a stable device identifier. Records use UTF-8 JSON Lines (.jsonl) in the app-local LocalDiagnostics folder.</value>
    </data>
    <data name="Shell_PrivacyExternalServices.Text" xml:space="preserve">
        <value>The app declares no network capability and adds no custom upload transport. Selecting a cloud-backed source, output or export location can nevertheless cause its provider to download or sync files, including photos or diagnostic exports. Windows and Microsoft Store may handle crash reporting, installation information or other diagnostics under your Windows choices and Microsoft's policies. These services operate separately from the app's local diagnostic log.</value>
    </data>
    <data name="Shell_PublisherPrivacyLink.Content" xml:space="preserve">
        <value>Hadden Industries privacy policy (opens browser)</value>
    </data>
    <data name="Shell_MicrosoftPrivacyLink.Content" xml:space="preserve">
        <value>Microsoft Privacy Statement (opens browser)</value>
    </data>
    <data name="Shell_PrivacyBrowserNotice.Text" xml:space="preserve">
        <value>The app's privacy notice is available here offline. Opening an external policy link launches your browser; the website receives ordinary connection information, such as your IP address, and may use cookies under its own policy. No photo or diagnostic export is attached to these links.</value>
    </data>
